{"summary":"All five amendments are technically sound. They address real gaps in regression coverage or diagnostics: two missing grazing assertions weaken failure triage, the water-change expectation is latent incorrectness hidden by a zero fixture, and the nitrification/alkalinity diagnostics should not mix mismatched quantities or units.","votes":[{"amendment_id":"fr-1-d5896362","decision":"accept","rationale":"The amendment identifies a concrete inconsistency in the test harness: this grazing test is the only one still using bare `assert!()` instead of the trace-dumping helper. That means a pathway failure loses the promised trace artifact, which violates the stated debugging contract and makes this scenario materially harder to diagnose."},{"amendment_id":"fr-1-0ed6fb15","decision":"accept","rationale":"This is a valid regression-coverage gap. `run_grazing()` currently checks only net conservation, so it can pass even if the grazing pathway is no longer exercised and the scenario is effectively inert. Adding the same minimum pathway checks used in the unit test makes the e2e scenario actually verify the intended behavior."},{"amendment_id":"fr-1-bd5aaa0c","decision":"accept","rationale":"The expected import formula should match the engine’s water-change accounting. Omitting `nitrite_mg_n_per_l` is currently masked by a zero-valued fixture, but it is still incorrect and would produce misleading expectations if the fixture changes. Including nitrite is the correct and robust fix."},{"amendment_id":"fr-1-026ed985","decision":"accept","rationale":"The amendment points out a real semantic mismatch: alkalinity consumption is coupled to TAN oxidation, not to nitrate appearance. A metric derived from `nob + comammox` nitrate production will diverge from the alkalinity charge whenever nitrite accumulates, making the trace internally misleading. The diagnostic should use TAN oxidation or be renamed to reflect nitrate production only."},{"amendment_id":"fr-1-bcb79444","decision":"accept","rationale":"If alkalinity is being asserted in the same helper path, reporting failures in `mg` is wrong because alkalinity is tracked in `meq`. Incorrect units in diagnostics are not cosmetic here; they directly mislead interpretation of deltas and tolerances. A unit-aware formatting path is the maintainable fix."}]}